.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH FREAD 3S
.SH NAME
fread, fwrite \- buffered binary input/output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B size_t fread(void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nitems, FILE *stream);
.PP
.B size_t fwrite(const void *ptr, size_t size, size_t nitems, FILE *stream);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fread
reads, into a block beginning at
.I ptr,
.I nitems
of data of
.I size
bytes each
from the named input
.IR stream .
It returns the number of items actually read.
.PP
.I Fwrite
appends at most
.I nitems
of data of
.I size
bytes each
beginning at
.I ptr
to the named output
.IR stream .
It returns the number of items actually written.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
read(2), write(2),
fopen(3),
getc(3), putc(3),
gets(3), puts(3),
printf(3), scanf(3), stdio(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Fread
and
.I fwrite
return
0
upon end of file or error.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B Size is a byte count, not a word count.
.B sizeof(int)
is
.BR 6 ,
six char-units, because six characters pack into one 48-bit word \- so
.B fread(buf, sizeof(int), n, f)
moves 6n bytes and not n of anything else.  This is the same arithmetic that
makes
.IR getw (3)
move six bytes rather than the PDP-11's two.
.PP
.B size_t is signed here.
That is a deliberate departure from C11 sec 7.19, recorded in include/README.md,
and it is why a mixed comparison against a difference of two counts behaves.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/fread.c, fwrite.c
